WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.920
In 1978, John Carpenter's "Halloween"&nbsp;
was released and became an instant hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.440
that spawned a huge horror franchise. It’s&nbsp;
a beloved classic, but not everything in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:15.920
the series was well-received. Here are&nbsp;
examples of questionable storylines.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:21.360
With over 10 entries in the "Halloween" canon, it&nbsp;
can be challenging to remember what has happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:27.360
before any given movie, and that's not entirely&nbsp;
the viewer's fault. "Halloween" debuted in 1978,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:33.760
and by the time "Halloween H20: 20 Years Later"&nbsp;
was released in 1998, there were six movies filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.240
with different characters. Instead of trying&nbsp;
to tie together the plots of all the films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:44.480
"Halloween H20" retconned everything outside of&nbsp;
the original movie and its sequel. In this version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:50.720
of events, films four through six never occurred,&nbsp;
meaning Laurie Strode never had a secret daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:56.800
who Michael Myers murdered. Instead, Laurie faked&nbsp;
her death, assumed a new identity, and had a son.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.960
Nine years later, Rob Zombie tried his&nbsp;
hand at making a "Halloween" movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.720
but he started over with a rebooted,&nbsp;
completely separate timeline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:10.240
creating his own version of the characters. When&nbsp;
the franchise returned in 2018 for a soft reboot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.520
it erased everything except the original&nbsp;
"Halloween" as if it were a direct sequel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:21.040
40 years in the making. It slyly addressed&nbsp;
this in the 2018 film when one character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:27.120
brings up plot points from 1981's "Halloween&nbsp;
2" and is bluntly told those were only rumors.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.960
Though the branching arcs and&nbsp;
characters may intimidate new viewers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.720
at the end of the day the series is&nbsp;
still relatively straightforward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.800
The Strode family is haunted by the trauma&nbsp;
of the events of the original "Halloween,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.560
and a relentless serial killer&nbsp;
terrorizes the town of Haddonfield.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.880
"Halloween: The Curse of Michael Myers"&nbsp;
starts with Michael hunting down Laurie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:52.000
Strode's 15-year-old daughter, Jamie, and her&nbsp;
newborn baby. Knowing Michael is after her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.360
Jamie calls into a radio show to&nbsp;
ask Michael's former psychiatrist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.320
Dr. Sam Loomis, for help. She then&nbsp;
immediately hides her baby. Soon after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:05.280
Michael finds and kills Jamie, but her baby&nbsp;
is found by a grown-up Tommy Doyle — the boy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.000
Laurie was babysitting in the first film.&nbsp;
Theorizing that Michael is intentionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.720
targeting members of his own family, Tommy does&nbsp;
his best to hide the infant from the killer.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.800
Being the nigh-unstoppable killing machine he is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:23.200
Michael eventually catches up with the baby,&nbsp;
and in the fabled "Producer's Cut" of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:28.880
a character tries to stop Michael from killing the&nbsp;
infant by suggesting it might be his child. Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:34.320
Laurie is Michael’s sister, this presents&nbsp;
the idea that he had a son with his niece.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.200
The implication of incest is thankfully omitted&nbsp;
in the theatrical cut, which without a doubt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.680
makes it the stronger version. Given the&nbsp;
uncomfortable nature of this reveal, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.720
probably better to stick to the wider release's&nbsp;
story and pretend incest never happened.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.640
The hectic opening of "Halloween: The Curse of&nbsp;
Michael Myers" should tip viewers off right away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:00.000
that this will not be an average entry in the&nbsp;
franchise. A woman giving birth is surrounded by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.280
doctors wheeling her gurney through an industrial&nbsp;
area, then into a stone-carved cavern lit with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.320
torches. Once the newborn is delivered, it's given&nbsp;
to a mysterious figure before the baby's mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:15.680
has a chance to hold him. The infant is then&nbsp;
placed on a stone altar and has a symbol drawn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:22.240
in red on its stomach. Surrounding the newborn&nbsp;
are three figures clad in black robes. It’s unlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:26.800
anything that has been seen in a "Halloween"&nbsp;
film until this point in the franchise.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:31.440
These robed figures are the movie's primary&nbsp;
villains, and it's eventually revealed that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:37.200
cult has found a way to control Michael using an&nbsp;
ancient rune of darkness known as the Thorn. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:41.920
claim seems to be confirmed in the producer's&nbsp;
cut when Tommy succeeds in halting Michael's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:47.120
rampage by using ancient runes of light. However,&nbsp;
thanks to difficulties with the production, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:52.800
cult storyline never fully took shape, and it's no&nbsp;
wonder that future sequels let it go altogether.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:59.680
Though Donald Pleasence died unexpectedly in 1995,&nbsp;
he remains a fixture of the "Halloween" franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.400
thanks to his character, Dr. Sam Loomis, and&nbsp;
his importance in the Michael Myers story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.434
Having been assigned to Michael Myers' case, Dr. Loomis had 15 years to treat the boy.

00:04:09.434 --> 00:04:11.147
By the time of the first film

00:04:11.147 --> 00:04:17.859
he decided Michael was no longer a man, but evil&nbsp;
in a human shape who had no chance of redemption.

00:04:17.859 --> 00:04:23.779
"I realized that what was living behind&nbsp;
that boy's eyes was purely and simply evil."

00:04:23.779 --> 00:04:29.059
From the moment Michael escapes, Dr. Loomis is&nbsp;
hellbent on taking Michael down by any means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.059 --> 00:04:34.019
necessary. This includes blowing himself&nbsp;
up, traumatizing children, giving a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.019 --> 00:04:40.659
locals inferred permission to patrol the town with&nbsp;
rifles, and holding police officers at gunpoint.

00:04:40.659 --> 00:04:45.619
Throughout the series, the authority figures&nbsp;
who interact with Loomis repeatedly dismiss the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.619 --> 00:04:50.419
doctor and underplay Michael's threat to their&nbsp;
communities. This isn't restricted to police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:55.059
chiefs, sheriffs, or government officials; even&nbsp;
the psychiatrist's colleagues believe he should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.059 --> 00:05:01.139
be receiving mental health counseling instead of&nbsp;
dispensing it. While he is undoubtedly obsessive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.139 --> 00:05:06.179
Dr. Loomis is almost always right whenever&nbsp;
he suspects Michael is at large. The only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.179 --> 00:05:11.539
time Dr. Loomis is repeatedly wrong is in&nbsp;
his guesses of how to kill Michael Myers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.539 --> 00:05:16.979
No matter how many times he tried, Loomis never&nbsp;
could quite get rid of his least-favorite patient.

00:05:17.699 --> 00:05:21.459
There are numerous shots throughout&nbsp;
the "Halloween" franchise of Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.459 --> 00:05:26.339
locking eyes with his victims in a menacing&nbsp;
fashion. These are always tense moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.339 --> 00:05:31.619
as they often signal the victim beginning to&nbsp;
understand the danger they're truly in. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.619 --> 00:05:38.499
shots also have a sort of calm-before-the-storm&nbsp;
vibe to them, focusing on stillness and inaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.499 --> 00:05:43.059
to better punctuate the frantic fleeing and&nbsp;
stabbing that is usually about to commence.

00:05:43.059 --> 00:05:47.459
However, one odd choice throughout the&nbsp;
franchise is the tendency to follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.459 --> 00:05:51.299
a specific sequence of shots&nbsp;
in these interactions. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.299 --> 00:05:55.459
the camera shows Michael staring at his&nbsp;
victim. What follows is a reverse shot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.459 --> 00:06:00.019
the victim looking at Michael. Then, the camera&nbsp;
cuts back to where the character is staring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.019 --> 00:06:05.299
only to show an empty, Michael-less space.&nbsp;
Sometimes, this gives Michael the appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.299 --> 00:06:10.259
of teleporting, as the character's gaze doesn't&nbsp;
tend to follow him to where he might have moved.

00:06:10.259 --> 00:06:14.419
While this is obviously an attempt by the&nbsp;
filmmakers to make Michael Myers more threatening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.419 --> 00:06:19.779
it actually raises more questions than it answers.&nbsp;
In a matter of seconds, Michael seemingly warps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.779 --> 00:06:24.099
across the room or safely off-screen, breaking&nbsp;
some of the tension that was previously in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.099 --> 00:06:28.899
scene. Though it may admittedly be awkward&nbsp;
to watch Michael walk from point A to point B&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.899 --> 00:06:33.459
in every instance, some creative editing may&nbsp;
help to make these moments more believable.

00:06:34.179 --> 00:06:38.899
By the time "Halloween: The Curse of Michael&nbsp;
Myers" happens, 17 years have passed since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.899 --> 00:06:42.979
Michael escaped from the institution in which&nbsp;
he was previously imprisoned for 15 years for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.979 --> 00:06:47.459
murdering his older sister. Upon obtaining&nbsp;
his freedom, he returned to his hometown of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.459 --> 00:06:51.939
Haddonfield to attack high school babysitter&nbsp;
Laurie Strode, who was later revealed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.939 --> 00:06:57.299
his younger sister. This means Michael's&nbsp;
murders have haunted the town for years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.299 --> 00:07:01.139
However, "The Curse of Michael Myers" is&nbsp;
the first time the holiday seems to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.139 --> 00:07:05.699
been curtailed by the local government ahead&nbsp;
of time. In fact, when the film takes place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.699 --> 00:07:09.699
Haddonfield residents are just beginning&nbsp;
to fight back against the restrictions.

00:07:09.699 --> 00:07:13.939
It seems odd that Haddonfield would go so far&nbsp;
as to restrict the public celebration of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.939 --> 00:07:19.299
holiday entirely, and especially odd that they&nbsp;
only thought to do it 30-some years after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.299 --> 00:07:24.019
first Myers murder. Aside from the two Halloweens&nbsp;
where Michael murdered one sister then attempted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.019 --> 00:07:29.379
to murder his other one, there have been several&nbsp;
incidents that probably should have been on local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.379 --> 00:07:34.339
officials' radars. In "Halloween 4: The Return of&nbsp;
Michael Myers," one of the local problems is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.339 --> 00:07:39.219
so many kids have dressed up as their town's&nbsp;
favorite serial killer that it's hard to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.219 --> 00:07:42.659
the real one. A similar problem&nbsp;
presents itself in the following film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.659 --> 00:07:46.419
"The Revenge of Michael Myers," where a&nbsp;
local kid dresses like Michael as a joke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.419 --> 00:07:50.979
allowing people to mistake the real killer for&nbsp;
a teenager messing with his friends. Instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.979 --> 00:07:56.579
banning the whole holiday, it seems like banning&nbsp;
the costume may have been a better choice.

00:07:57.299 --> 00:08:00.899
Rob Zombie's "Halloween" films&nbsp;
are contentious among fans of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.899 --> 00:08:05.779
the franchise. While some admire the&nbsp;
depth of backstory for Michael Myers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.779 --> 00:08:11.539
others find that the characterization diminishes&nbsp;
his impact and is unnecessary. However, what most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.539 --> 00:08:17.859
fans agree on is that Rob Zombie's "Halloween 2"&nbsp;
is one of the most bizarre entries in the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.859 --> 00:08:24.499
feeling much more like a hyper-stylized Rob&nbsp;
Zombie acid horror trip than a "Halloween" film.

00:08:24.499 --> 00:08:30.339
To some extent, Zombie's "Halloween 2" follows&nbsp;
similar story beats to the original "Halloween 2."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.339 --> 00:08:34.579
Both films confirm that Laurie Strode&nbsp;
and Michael Myers are siblings, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.579 --> 00:08:38.339
unlike the original, Zombie's&nbsp;
"Halloween 2" focuses more on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.339 --> 00:08:42.579
psychological aspect of the characters&nbsp;
and delves into surrealist territory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.579 --> 00:08:47.139
The film features several hallucinations&nbsp;
that link the siblings' minds, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.139 --> 00:08:52.259
scenes in which Laurie begins to envision&nbsp;
herself carrying out Michael's past murders.

00:08:52.259 --> 00:08:56.579
Eventually, Laurie is sent to an institution&nbsp;
similar to the one that held her brother for 15&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.579 --> 00:09:02.499
years. Here, we see the most outlandish imagery&nbsp;
in the entirety of the "Halloween" franchise:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.499 --> 00:09:07.619
a hallucination of a white horse that feels more&nbsp;
akin to a fantasy film than something that should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.619 --> 00:09:12.899
be in "Halloween 2." Overall, Zombie's "Halloween&nbsp;
2" is one of the oddest films in the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.899 --> 00:09:16.579
and the abstract nature of the&nbsp;
movie is often forgotten or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.579 --> 00:09:20.819
ignored when discussing some of the&nbsp;
latter, more disliked, films in the series.

00:09:21.539 --> 00:09:25.219
One of the most effective ways to&nbsp;
make a horror film scary is through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.219 --> 00:09:29.939
clever sound design. Most people familiar&nbsp;
with jump scares know that music and sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.939 --> 00:09:33.459
effects are frequently used to get&nbsp;
a reaction out of audience members.

00:09:33.459 --> 00:09:39.299
Beyond jump scares and the use of a film's score,&nbsp;
sound effects that aren't made to scare in and of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.299 --> 00:09:44.659
themselves can be hugely effective in ratcheting&nbsp;
up the tension of any given scene. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.659 --> 00:09:49.619
the metallic "shing" of a knife as it's drawn&nbsp;
helps reinforce its sharpness in the mind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.619 --> 00:09:54.099
audience members, and the loud "thwack" that&nbsp;
rings out when that same blade is plunged into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.099 --> 00:09:59.379
its target can make the impact seem much&nbsp;
more terrifying than the visual alone.

00:09:59.379 --> 00:10:04.979
With so much potential to enhance the mood of&nbsp;
a scene, the misuse of sound effects is often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.979 --> 00:10:09.219
glaring and can take the viewer right out&nbsp;
of the movie. While the "Halloween" films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.219 --> 00:10:15.459
generally use sound effects well, there are a few&nbsp;
unforgettable instances where they feel distinctly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.459 --> 00:10:21.059
out of place. The best example of this is found&nbsp;
in "Halloween 5: The Revenge of Michael Myers."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.059 --> 00:10:25.779
During a comedic scene between two police&nbsp;
officers, a series of clown sound effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.779 --> 00:10:30.339
including honking, are played during&nbsp;
their banter without any explanation.

00:10:30.339 --> 00:10:32.899
"Fortunately we're lousy cops... Sorry."

00:10:33.379 --> 00:10:36.739
"Halloween: Resurrection" features&nbsp;
a group of teens wandering around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.739 --> 00:10:41.699
the abandoned Myers house on Halloween night&nbsp;
as part of an internet livestream. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.699 --> 00:10:47.299
reality show functions by having all the cameras&nbsp;
accessible for audiences to watch at any time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.299 --> 00:10:51.699
and the contestant that most viewers&nbsp;
choose to watch is the winner. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.699 --> 00:10:56.099
little do the participants or the show's&nbsp;
producers know that Michael Myers has made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.099 --> 00:11:01.139
his way back to his childhood home, and he’s&nbsp;
ready to kill anyone he gets his hands on.

00:11:01.139 --> 00:11:05.219
When it becomes clear to audiences that&nbsp;
contestants are actually being murdered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.219 --> 00:11:07.779
and aren't playing pranks on the others for views,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.779 --> 00:11:12.579
the mood inside and outside the house&nbsp;
becomes less lighthearted. Luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.579 --> 00:11:17.779
one contestant has a friend watching who texts her&nbsp;
Michael's location while checking other cameras.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.779 --> 00:11:22.659
This ends up saving her life a couple of&nbsp;
times and even earns the guy a shoutout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.659 --> 00:11:27.779
once reporters show up to get a statement from the&nbsp;
survivor, who’s still wrapped in a shock blanket.

00:11:27.779 --> 00:11:32.579
While this works well for the characters&nbsp;
in the film, it seems a bit suspect that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.579 --> 00:11:38.659
a livestream with upwards of a dozen cameras&nbsp;
all broadcasting live would have no lag while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.659 --> 00:11:44.099
operating within a small Illinois town's 2002&nbsp;
internet infrastructure. Aside from having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.099 --> 00:11:48.579
instantaneous uploading, there also appears&nbsp;
to be no issue with buffering or loading on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.579 --> 00:11:54.179
the viewing end as up-to-the-second info on&nbsp;
Michael is sent via text. Even if the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.179 --> 00:12:00.659
was done today, there would likely be at least&nbsp;
a several-second-long delay for viewers at home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.659 --> 00:12:05.379
and certainly, a few more seconds&nbsp;
to wait for a text message to send.

